import{_ as t,o as a,a as o,d as n}from"./chunks/framework.IehvnstE.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Mods","description":"Mods in Stuzhik: the Modrinth and CurseForge catalog, dependencies only of what you install, «Not for this build», a graph with actions, «Overlaps» (mixin and recipe clashes), pinned versions and auto-update.","frontmatter":{"description":"Mods in Stuzhik: the Modrinth and CurseForge catalog, dependencies only of what you install, «Not for this build», a graph with actions, «Overlaps» (mixin and recipe clashes), pinned versions and auto-update.","head":[["meta",{"name":"keywords","content":"minecraft mods, stuzhik, how to install minecraft mods, modrinth, curseforge, forge mods, fabric mods, minecraft mod conflicts, minecraft mod dependencies, recipe conflict, mixin conflict"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/guide/mods.md","filePath":"en/guide/mods.md"}'),s={name:"en/guide/mods.md"};function i(r,e,d,l,h,c){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="mods" tabindex="-1">Mods <a class="header-anchor" href="#mods" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Mods&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Find and install a mod, change its version, update it, sort out dependencies and conflicts: all of it is on the «Mods» tab of the build page. At the top are two modes: «Installed» and «Catalog».</p><h2 id="catalog" tabindex="-1">Catalog <a class="header-anchor" href="#catalog" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Catalog&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The catalog searches Modrinth and CurseForge <strong>already narrowed to the build&#39;s game version and loader</strong>; «Show incompatible» lifts that filter. «Categories» narrow the search, «Add to favorites» keeps a mod for later. A list or tiles is picked with a button in the catalog&#39;s toolbar and remembered for every catalog.</p><p><strong>«Install» takes the version for the build</strong>: the one that fits next to the mods already installed. While a mod installs, its card shows «Picking a version…» and «Downloading · N%», the cross «Cancel install» stops it. Everything being installed into the build also shows on the «Installed» side, as an «Installing» block above the list.</p><p><strong>A mod comes with only its own dependencies.</strong> Missing dependencies of mods you did not touch are installed apart, with «Install all missing» on the «Dependencies» tab.</p><p>Your own <code>.jar</code> can be dropped onto the window or added with «Add local mod (.jar file)».</p><h2 id="the-mod-card" tabindex="-1">The mod card <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-mod-card" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The mod card&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A click on a mod opens its card: the description, every version and the dependencies.</p><ul><li><strong>Changing the version</strong>: pick a version right in the list, no need to remove and reinstall. «Hide incompatible» drops the versions that will not fit this game and loader</li><li><strong>The graph</strong>: what depends on what and what conflicts with what</li></ul><h2 id="the-mod-list" tabindex="-1">The mod list <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-mod-list" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The mod list&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Turn on and off</strong>: the switch in the row; a disabled mod stays as <code>.jar.disabled</code> and does not load</li><li><strong>Updates</strong>: the button above the list checks for new versions for this build and shows what will update. If a download fails, the old file stays</li><li><strong>Pin the version</strong>: the pin in the row or the item in the right-click menu. Updates, auto-update and a modpack update leave a pinned version alone</li><li><strong>Auto-update</strong>: the icon in a mod&#39;s row: a found update installs by itself when updates are checked. Whether new mods get it is decided in «Settings → Mods»</li><li><strong>Delete</strong>: in the mod&#39;s menu; several mods: select them and delete at once. Before deleting you see what depends on it</li></ul><p><strong>Changes show at once</strong>, wherever they happen: a mod disabled in the log analysis, updated by a modpack or dropped into the folder as a file appears in the list without a reload.</p><h2 id="mod-conflicts" tabindex="-1">Dependencies and conflicts <a class="header-anchor" href="#mod-conflicts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dependencies and conflicts {#mod-conflicts}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The «Dependencies» tab gathers everything in the build&#39;s way:</p><ul><li><strong>Missing dependencies</strong> and <strong>wrong versions</strong>: «Install all missing» installs them with one button</li><li><strong>Incompatible pairs</strong>: mods that do not work together</li><li><strong>«Not for this build»</strong>: a mod for another game version or another loader («AppleSkin for Minecraft 1.21.11, but the build is on 1.21.1»), with a «Disable» button. The pre-launch check shows the same</li></ul><p>The graph shows this as a picture, and <strong>in it you can also disable or delete a mod or install its missing dependency</strong>. Disabling and deleting show the same «what will break» window, and a missing dependency has its own button: only it and what it needs itself are installed. The nodes stay where they are.</p><h2 id="overlaps" tabindex="-1">Overlaps <a class="header-anchor" href="#overlaps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Overlaps {#overlaps}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The «Overlaps» tab in «Dependencies» shows <strong>where mods get in each other&#39;s way, before the game is even started</strong>: it reads the jar files of the enabled mods.</p><ul><li><strong>Mixin clashes</strong>: two mods replace the same game method whole or redirect the same call, so one change will not apply</li><li><strong>The same game methods</strong>: several mods change one method. Usually that works, but <strong>if the game crashes there, start with them</strong>. On Fabric and Quilt methods carry readable names (<code>PalettedContainer.acquire</code>, not <code>class_2841.method_12334</code>)</li><li><strong>One craft, different items</strong>: two mods give different items for the same ingredients, and the game takes only one recipe. You see the crafting grid or the furnace with icons and every result variant <ul><li>KubeJS, CraftTweaker and Polymorph are taken into account when the build has them</li><li>Create&#39;s machines are checked the same way: crushing wheels, millstone, press, fan, the mixer and the press over a basin (fluids included), deployer, spout, mechanical crafter</li><li>each finding says whether the other item can be had at all: a basin&#39;s filter picks it, the crafting table and the furnaces take a chooser mod like Polymorph when the build has one, while the press, the wheels and the fan give what they give. Those findings come first</li><li>the mechanical saw is not listed: several results are its design, it cycles through them</li></ul></li><li><strong>Same materials</strong> and <strong>ore generated twice</strong>: tin, steel, lead from several mods</li><li><strong>Libraries at different versions</strong>: mods embed one library, the loader keeps one</li></ul><p>«Copy report» gives all of it as text, to send to a mod&#39;s or a modpack&#39;s author.</p><h2 id="built-in-update-checks" tabindex="-1">Built-in update checks <a class="header-anchor" href="#built-in-update-checks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Built-in update checks&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Mod Menu, Iris, Distant Horizons, Forge and NeoForge can announce new versions at every start by themselves, and Distant Horizons can download its update on its own. <strong>The launcher turns these checks off by default</strong>: it handles mod updates. The switch is in «Settings → Mods → Mod integrations». One build can have its own choice in «Edit», and which builds do not follow the common setting shows in «Settings → Launch → Per-build settings».</p><h2 id="performance" tabindex="-1">Performance <a class="header-anchor" href="#performance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Performance {#performance}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The «Performance» tab (under «More» on the build page) shows memory and CPU while the game runs. With the Spark mod it also shows <strong>what each mod costs</strong> («Impact»), bottlenecks and recommendations. Spark installs with a button there.</p><h2 id="where-the-mods-are" tabindex="-1">Where the mods are <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-the-mods-are" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where the mods are&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In the build&#39;s folder: <code>mods</code> inside <code>instances/&lt;build&gt;</code> of the launcher&#39;s data folder. Where that is and how to move it: <a href="/en/guide/instances.html#data-location">Builds → Where the data is</a>.</p><h2 id="if-something-is-wrong" tabindex="-1">If something is wrong <a class="header-anchor" href="#if-something-is-wrong" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;If something is wrong&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>A mod does not show up in the game.</strong> Look at the «Dependencies» tab: the mod may be «Not for this build» (another game version or loader), or it lacks a dependency. And check that it is enabled.</p><p><strong>The game crashed after installing a mod.</strong> The <a href="/en/guide/log-analyzer.html">Log Analyzer</a> names the culprit and offers to update or disable it. To go back to the previous version: the mod&#39;s card, the version list.</p><p><strong>A mod does not update.</strong> Its version may be pinned (the pin in the row), or there is no new version for your game version yet.</p>',31)])])}const p=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
